.TH "SKARN-RESTORE" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.38.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-restore \- Restore a session
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBrestore\fR \fIsession\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print the shell command that resumes a session, so `eval $(skarn restore
<session>)` returns you to its working directory. When the recorded working
directory or session id carries a byte the running shell would read back as
something else, skarn refuses to render the command: stdout stays empty, the
refusal is named on stderr, and restore exits 1. When the recorded working
directory is not a local absolute path for this operating system, a directory
on it cannot be checked, or a link on it points to a network path, the command
is printed without `cd <dir> &&` (the Cursor and VS Code forms without the
workspace argument), and one stderr line says the directory was not used
without naming it.
.PP
Restore a session by id. The resume command is printed from the raw session,
because a masked working directory would not be a usable command: when the
recorded working directory carries a detected secret, restore refuses with
exit 1, says on stderr that the resume command cannot be printed, and prints
nothing to stdout. The match hint on stderr masks the session id, project,
branch and assistant name, and a comment line that names a session shows its
id masked the same way. When the recorded working directory is not a local
absolute path for this operating system (a network, device or relative path, a
path written for the other operating system, or one with a control character),
a directory on it cannot be checked, or a link on it points to a network path,
restore prints the resume command without `cd <dir> &&`, the Gemini form
prints `gemini \-\-list\-sessions` because its resume index counts the
sessions of the current directory, the Grok prompt\-log form prints `grok
sessions` without it, and the Cursor and VS Code forms print `cursor` and
`code` without the workspace argument; one stderr line says the recorded
directory was not used and does not repeat it. A directory on it that passes
through a link the session link check refuses for another reason (a link it
cannot resolve, or a component it cannot check for links) takes the same
fallbacks with a different stderr line: that line names the recorded
directory, cut at the label length as the evidence\-machine line is, and says
Skarn did not follow a link on it or could not check a component of it for
links. The secret refusal and the shell\-byte refusal still exit 1.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Session id to operate on (prefix\-matched); equivalent to the positional
argument.
.TP
\fB\-\-evidence\-home\fR \fIdir\fR
Read a copied home's default session stores (Pro). For configuration
discovery, Skarn reads the copied home's own assistant configuration. Skarn
looks where this machine's operating system keeps each store, so a copy taken
on another operating system can show fewer session or configuration rows. The
Compliance API export root resolves under dir too, and reading it still needs
Enterprise. Session discovery ignores store overrides, XDG_DATA_HOME,
XDG_CONFIG_HOME and SKARN_COMPLIANCE_EXPORT_DIR. The license, EULA record,
~/.skarn.json, caches and outputs stay with the examiner. Skarn ignores the
copied .skarn.json. Skarn resolves ~/ in sessions_path under dir and reports
skipped absolute paths. All history is in scope unless you specify \-\-hours,
\-\-since or \-\-until. Only an explicit \-\-baseline applies. Assess reads
the copied home's saved permission rules for saved_approvals when in scope.
Vet reads only home\-layer configuration and refuses \-\-all\-projects and
\-\-project\-only. Inventory reads sessions and home\-layer configuration. Its
\-\-project filter applies only to sessions. Check writes the same
skarn.inventory line with \-\-format ndjson and refuses \-\-drop,
\-\-audit\-verify and \-\-baseline\-merge. Restore omits cd and names the
evidence machine's unchecked directory on stderr. The session scan skips
transcript path checks, reads derived metadata only under dir and follows no
link below the evidence home: each refused link counts once as an unreadable
store, and one stderr line per reason gives the count. Configuration discovery
in vet and inventory follows no symbolic link below dir either. Both accept a
plain UNC share as the evidence home and exit 6 for a refused network or
device spelling or a control character. Skarn refuses outputs under dir and
homes that contain the examiner's home or overlap Skarn's config or cache
directories (exit 1). Missing, unreadable or non\-directory paths, paths
containing *, and paths that pass through a link to a network path, a link
Skarn cannot resolve or a component Skarn cannot check for links cause exit 6;
the stderr line names the link. Licensing failures exit 7 (no usable license)
or 5 (insufficient tier) before evidence access. Input files can reside
anywhere. JSON, SARIF, NDJSON and the audit record have no dedicated
evidence\-home field. Finding paths show the directory. The evidence pack
names it on one line. For check, \-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error fails incomplete
scans. Requires a Pro license.
